//! The VeriSilicon VIP9000 neural accelerator, behind the vendor kernel's `vipcore` node. No vendor
//! library: what runs on the block (a compiled program) is the caller's bytes, which Lemnos never
//! reads, parses or compiles. The vendor's user-space runtime, its model tools and its compiled
//! format are not used, read or followed.
//!
//! - [`uapi`]: the node's request number, commands, flags and the structures a run takes, pinned
//!   against the C compiler by `layout.rs` (generated by `layout-check/run.sh`).
//! - [`request`]: checked builders for the requests a run needs, each refusing the numbers the
//!   kernel takes unchecked, and [`request::withheld`]: why every other command is never sent.
//! - [`confine`]: what the kernel driver lets a job reach, as read from its source, and the check
//!   that a job's table maps only the job's own buffers.
//! - [`Vip9000`]: the `npu/1` contract over a [`Device`], refusing to load programs while a job
//!   could reach past its buffers; the check backs off (250 ms doubling to an 8 s cap) and never
//!   gives up.
//!
//! # What is not here, and why
//!
//! No job runs. A program can name any device address, so a job is confined only if the block's
//! address translation maps nothing but that job's buffers while it runs. The vendor's `vipcore`
//! cannot give that from user space: the block has no system IOMMU, its own MMU has one page table
//! for every opener of the node, the kernel keeps its shared heap and on-chip memories mapped
//! there from start-up, and any opener may write any register offset or map any physical page.
//! [`confine`] lists each fact with its source, and what a Lemnos kernel path for the block would
//! need to close it. So [`Npu::load`](lemnos_contract_npu::Npu::load) answers `NOT_SUPPORTED`, and
//! no Linux [`Device`] (and no `-sys` crate issuing the request) is built: the tests run the
//! driver and the checks against models of the kernel's mapping behaviour.
//!
//! Hardware status: untested on board. Written from the vendor driver's source; the check is
//! `testing/board-runs/E33.md`.
//!
//! Provenance: new. Every fact is from the Radxa `allwinner-bsp` tree (github.com/radxa/
//! allwinner-bsp) at commit `a842bb8dc74608ba13975c97c31b9518f1976c01` (branch
//! `cubie-aiot-v1.5.0`): `drivers/npu/aw_nna_vip/vip2/` (VeriSilicon, dual MIT and GPL, used under
//! MIT), `drivers/npu/aw_nna_galcore/` (the vendor's other driver for the same compatible string),
//! `drivers/npu/aw_nna_vip/Kconfig` and `configs/linux-6.6/sun60iw2p1.dtsi`; each module names
//! its files. Interface facts only are transcribed; no code is copied. The shape follows
//! `drivers/rk-mpp` and `drivers/rkaiisp`.

